P1: PostgreSQL instances per Claims app (a corrected count)

timeseries up = avg(postgres.uptime, scalar: true), by:{device.name, device}, from:-2h
| fieldsAdd hostShort = lower(splitString(device.name, ".")[0])
| lookup [
    fetch dt.entity.host
    | fields hostName = entity.name, tags
    | expand t = tags
    | filter startsWith(t, "APMID:")
    | fieldsAdd apmid = splitString(t, "APMID:")[1],
                hostShort = lower(splitString(hostName, ".")[0])
    | filter in(apmid, array($ClaimsApps))
    | summarize claimsApps = collectDistinct(apmid), by:{hostShort}
  ], sourceField: hostShort, lookupField: hostShort, prefix: "h."
| filter isNotNull(h.claimsApps)
| expand apmid = h.claimsApps
| summarize pgInstances = countDistinctExact(device),
            pgHosts     = countDistinctExact(device.name),
            by:{apmid}
| sort pgInstances desc


What to check: the number of rows is the real count of apps (the 17 may change). If many apps have the same pgInstances, those hosts are shared between apps.

P2: Which PostgreSQL metrics exist

fetch metric.series, from:-2h
| filter startsWith(metric.key, "postgres.")
| summarize series = count(), by:{metric.key}
| sort series desc


This shows whether there are metrics for replication, connections, deadlocks and database size. I haven't seen the full list.

